course: medicine
lesson: 36
title: Heart and lungs when you run
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you run, your leg muscles burn their fuel with oxygen, and suddenly they need far more of it than when you sit on the sofa.
explain: So the heart, a muscle about the size of your fist near the middle of your chest, beats faster to push more blood round, and you breathe faster and deeper to bring oxygen in and blow carbon dioxide out.
explain: Picture the last lap on sports day: face red, chest heaving, heart thumping in your ears. That is not your body breaking down, it is your body keeping up.
explain: Train often and the heart muscle gets stronger and pushes more blood with every beat, so a fit heart can beat more slowly at rest.
explain: Puffing is normal; chest pain, fainting or a collapse are not, and someone who collapses and is not breathing normally needs 999 and a defibrillator straight away.
fact: Working muscles need much more oxygen, so the heart beats faster and you breathe faster and deeper; being out of breath while running is normal.
fact: A child's resting pulse is often about 70 to 100 beats a minute, and it can climb to about 160 to 190 when running hard.
fact: You can feel your pulse on the thumb side of either wrist or at the side of the neck; count the beats for 15 seconds and multiply by 4.
fact: The heart sits near the middle of the chest, tipped a little to the left, and the pulse can be felt on both wrists.
fact: Training makes the heart stronger so it pumps more blood per beat; a fit adult's resting pulse of around 50 to 60 is normal for them.
fact: An adult heart at rest beats roughly 100,000 times a day (a lakh, in Indian English); exercise does not use up heartbeats, and over time it lowers the resting rate.
fact: At hard effort, breathing through the mouth as well as the nose lets in much more air, and that is fine.
fact: A red face and sweat are the body letting heat out, as blood vessels near the skin widen; dizziness, feeling sick or feeling confused mean stop and tell an adult.
fact: The talk test: if you can talk but not sing you are working moderately; if you can only gasp a few words you are working hard; both count as exercise.
fact: Nerves release adrenaline, which speeds up the heart and breathing even when you stand still, for example before a penalty.
fact: With their reliever inhaler, which opens narrowed airways, and an asthma plan, children with asthma can play almost any sport, and many Olympic athletes have asthma; if the inhaler doesn't help, an adult calls 999.
fact: After you stop, the heart rate falls fast in the first minute or two, and a slow walk helps you feel less dizzy than flopping down at once.
fact: Chest pain or fainting during sport is never normal; a person who collapses and is not breathing normally needs 999 and a defibrillator, which is how footballer Christian Eriksen was saved at Euro 2020, played in 2021.
exercise: Aisha from Leeds, two laps in: "I'm blowin' like a steam train, our kid." Why is she puffing? | Her muscles need more oxygen, so she breathes faster | Her lungs have stopped working properly for a bit | The air on the field is too thin | She caught a cold from the running
exercise: Resting about 80 beats a minute, running about 160. How many more beats each minute? | 80 | 240 | 2 | 40
exercise: Where can you feel your pulse? | On the thumb side of either wrist, or the neck | Only on the left wrist, because it's next to the heart | Only on the chest, never on a wrist | Only on the ankle, after running
exercise: Liam in Dublin counts 30 beats in 15 seconds. How many beats a minute is that? | 120 | 45 | 60 | 450
exercise: Zoe's pulse on the sofa is about 85, her marathon-running dad's about 55. Why is his slower? | Training made his heart stronger, so it pumps more each beat | His heart is slowly wearing out from all that running and racing | A slow pulse always means a heart is ill | Grown-ups' hearts only beat when they move
exercise: Roughly how many times a day does an adult heart beat at rest? | About 100,000 | About 1,000 or so | About 10 million | About 500 a day
exercise: Priya in Mumbai: "My heart beats a lakh times a day, so cricket wastes them?" What's true? | No, exercise lowers the resting rate over time | Yes, everyone gets a fixed number of beats for their whole life | Yes, so sporty people run out sooner | Only sprinting wastes them, jogging doesn't
exercise: Kai's coach in Auckland: "Nose breathing only, mate!" Kai is gasping up a hill. What's true? | At hard effort, breathing through the mouth too is fine | Mouth breathing while running dries out and harms the lungs | Nose-only breathing always lets in more air | Gasping means he must give up sport
exercise: Jayden in Texas comes off the court scarlet and dripping. Why so red? | Blood vessels near the skin widen to let heat out | He is certain to faint in a minute | His blood has got too hot and is close to boiling | His heart has stopped pumping blood properly round the body
exercise: Which signs during exercise mean stop and tell an adult? | Feeling dizzy, sick or confused | A red face and lots of sweat running down | Fast breathing and a fast, strong pulse | Tired, heavy legs on the last lap
exercise: Grace in Cape Town chats while jogging, then can only gasp after sprints. What does the talk test say? | Jogging is moderate, sprinting is hard, both count | Only the sprinting counts as exercise | Chatting means she isn't really exercising at all, just walking fast | Gasping means she should see a doctor
exercise: Ollie has asthma. Can he play striker? | Yes, with his reliever inhaler and a plan | No, asthma means no running sport ever again | Only in goal, where there's hardly any running | Only if he leaves the inhaler at home on match days
exercise: Seán stands still over a penalty, heart hammering. Why? | Adrenaline is getting his body ready to act | His heart is failing under all the pressure | He is too weak for big matches | He must have run too much before
exercise: Mei in Vancouver drops flat straight after the bleep test. What's better? | A slow walk for a couple of minutes first | Lying totally still for an hour | Sprinting one more lap to clear the legs | Gulping a fizzy drink lying down
exercise: Mei's pulse is 170 right after the test and 130 a minute later. How far did it fall? | 40 beats a minute | 300 beats a minute | 13 beats a minute | 4 beats a minute
exercise: Ruby sees a man collapse in the park, not breathing normally. Someone shouts "He's just tired!" What's right? | Call 999 and get a defibrillator | Leave him on the grass to sleep it off | Give him a drink of water and sit him up | Walk on, it's none of her business
test: "Bro my heart's at 180 after the run, I'm lowkey cooked fr" — he's a healthy kid. What's the truth? | A very fast heartbeat while running is normal | 180 means his heart is about to burst out of his chest | He must never run again | Fast beats use up his heart's life
test: Mate: "You only got a pulse on your left wrist cos that's where your heart is, innit." Right? | No, the heart is near the middle and both wrists have a pulse | Yes, the right wrist has no pulse at all, it's too far from the heart | Yes, but only in left-handed people | No, the pulse is only in the ankle
test: "Shut up and run. If you can still talk you ain't trying." Fair? | No, moderate effort where you can talk counts too | Yes, only gasping for breath counts as real exercise | Yes, talking while running is dangerous | No, exercise only counts in a gym
test: What would happen to your resting pulse if you trained a few times a week for some months? | It would likely get a bit slower | It would stop for good | It would double at rest | It could never change at all, whatever you did
test: Grandad, back in his day: "Breathe through your nose, son, open mouth lets the cold in." On a steep hill? | Breathing through the mouth too is fine at hard effort | Mouth breathing on a cold hill makes you catch a cold | Mouth breathing shrinks the lungs | The nose lets in more air when sprinting
test: "Ollie's puffer is basically cheating, lol." What does a reliever inhaler really do? | Opens narrowed airways so he breathes normally | Makes him faster than anyone else on the pitch | It's a drug that is banned for all children in sport | Nothing, it's only for show
test: In a cartoon a runner's heart bursts out of his chest after a race. Real life? | The heart beats fast but stays put and slows down after | Hearts can pop out a little way after very long races | Hearts stop when you run too fast | Running makes the heart shrink
test: "He's knackered, leave him" — but the man on the grass isn't breathing normally. What now? | It's an emergency: 999 and a defibrillator | Leave him, he's only tired from the run, he'll be fine | Shake him hard until he wakes up again by himself | Wait twenty minutes to see if he wakes, then call
test: Zoe's mate: "Your dad's pulse is 55?? Dead slow, he's dying, no cap." Right? | No, a slow resting pulse is normal in fit people | Yes, under 60 at rest always means a serious illness | Yes, he should stop exercising | No, grown-ups have no pulse at rest
test: Halfway through a match you get chest pain and feel faint. What do you do? | Stop and tell an adult straight away | Run it off, it's only nerves | Drink a sports drink and play on | Ignore it till the final whistle
test: Seán feels he's gonna boke (be sick) before his penalty, heart going like the clappers. Best move? | One slow breath out, pick a spot, shoot | Run a lap first to calm his heart down | Refuse to take it, it's dangerous | Hold his breath until the kick
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the rate of respiration increases during vigorous running. | The working muscles require an increased supply of oxygen | The lungs contract and gradually lose their capacity to hold air | The heart ceases to circulate the blood | The air becomes deficient in oxygen
exam: It is given that a pupil counts thirty-five pulsations within an interval of fifteen seconds. Determine the corresponding rate per minute. | One hundred and forty | Fifty | Seventy | Five hundred and twenty-five
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a slow resting pulse in a trained adult. | It is a normal consequence of a strengthened heart | It invariably denotes cardiac disease of a serious character | It indicates that heartbeats have been exhausted | It signifies imminent collapse
exam: Whereas breathlessness during exertion is normal, ascertain which occurrence requires an immediate call to the emergency services. | A collapse in which the person is not breathing normally | A reddened and perspiring face following a sprint | A rapid pulse at the conclusion of a race | Perspiration during a match
